findings.recommendation:
Sanitise names inside filterToken before they enter a variant, which fixes both the injection and the width accounting in one place:

```go
// sanitizeIndicatorName strips control runes so a server-supplied column name cannot
// carry escape sequences into the cmdline and cannot break the rune-width accounting
// that composeCmdline's truncation ladder depends on.
func sanitizeIndicatorName(s string) string {
    return strings.Map(func(r rune) rune {
        if r < 0x20 || r == 0x7f {
            return -1
        }
        return r
    }, s)
}
```

Apply it at `names = append(names, sanitizeIndicatorName(cols[i]))`, and add a unit test asserting that a name containing "\x1b[31m" produces a token with no 0x1b. Consider the same treatment for printHeaderCell as separate pre-existing hardening — out of scope for this feature, worth one line in tech-debt.

Give the timer goroutine an exit path tied to the UI lifetime. mainLoop already owns a per-iteration ctx (top/ui.go:35); expose its Done channel to writeCmdline (or hang it off app) and gate the wait:

```go
t := time.NewTimer(2 * time.Second)
go func() {
    select {
    case <-t.C:
        g.Update(restore)
    case <-done:
        t.Stop()
    }
}()
```

This is best-effort — a teardown between `<-t.C` and the enqueue still blocks — but it closes the common case and makes the lifetime explicit. Separately, add `g.Close()` to mainLoop's restart path so the abandoned Gui at least releases termbox; record the remaining window as a known limitation in the Risks table alongside the existing "dialog keeps its coordinates on resize" entry.

Make the invariant explicit and testable at the call site in dialogOpen:

```go
x1 := maxX - 1
x0 := dialogInputX0(utf8.RuneCountInString(line), maxX, minDialogInputWidth)
if x0 >= x1 {
    // Terminal too narrow to draw a dialog at all — decline the dialog instead of
    // handing SetView invalid dimensions, which would tear down and rebuild the UI.
    printCmdline(g, "terminal too narrow for this dialog")
    return nil
}
v, err := g.SetView("dialog", x0, cmdlineY0, x1, cmdlineY1)
```

Add the maxX <= 0 and maxX == 1 cases to the dialogInputX0 test table listed in Testing Strategy so the boundary is pinned, and state in Decision 3 that the guard — not the clamp arithmetic — is what makes the teardown unreachable.

Either bound the probe or bound the input. Cheapest is a loop cap in scrollOffsetFor with a comment stating why:

```go
// maxScrollProbes bounds the probe loop. The widest built-in screen has ~19 columns; a
// larger Ncols can only come from a server that is not answering pgcenter's own queries,
// and a linear-in-n^2 walk on the UI goroutine is not worth serving it.
const maxScrollProbes = 256
```

Stop probing past that and return the current offset unchanged (the window simply does not move — the pre-feature behaviour). Add a unit-test row with a large ncols asserting the function returns promptly. Alternatively add a column-count sanity bound to PGresult.validate, which would cover every consumer rather than just this one — but that is a wider change than this feature warrants.
